WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:20.550
This amoeba is making its home out of a drop
of water, taken from a larger sample and then

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:28.779
sandwiched between a glass slide and a coverslip
before being placed onto a microscope, oh

00:00:28.779 --> 00:00:30.330
so carefully.

00:00:30.330 --> 00:00:38.050
And perched above, is us, peering down through
the lens to see a magnified version of its

00:00:38.050 --> 00:00:40.300
daily life.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:46.199
And coming from underneath the slide is white
light, often passing through microbial bodies

00:00:46.199 --> 00:00:51.970
so that they appear transparent, like moving
outlines.

00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:58.750
But white light is a fracturable thing, made
up of different wavelengths that can interact

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:04.190
in their own particular ways with the world
they encounter, absorbed by some materials

00:01:04.190 --> 00:01:06.920
and reflected by others.

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:13.110
For wavelengths that lie in the visible spectrum,
that reflected light gives us color.

00:01:13.110 --> 00:01:22.140
Like the inside of this ciliate, its clear
body rendered kaleidoscopic following a colorful meal.

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:30.370
We often link color to perception: how we
see it, how it makes us feel, how we pick

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:34.180
and choose our favorites.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:38.330
Colors signal, not just like traffic lights,
but in nature.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:45.390
Feathers that attract, bright markings that
warn, and patterns that camouflage.

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:52.170
But setting aside our own voyeurism, there
are no complex eyes on you when you’re microscopic.

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:58.700
The colors that emerge are entirely incidental,
in a sense stripped down to their fundamental

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:05.670
natures: the product of light absorbing and
reflecting at different wavelengths, these

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:11.930
colors do not exist to communicate or signal,
they’re just the color they are, and when

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:19.280
we find beauty here, it’s not because there’s
any advantage to that beauty, it’s just luck.

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:24.819
Maybe the easiest color to translate from
what we see on a daily basis to what we observe

00:02:24.819 --> 00:02:32.219
under the microscope is green, the color of
photosynthesis, and thus of many plants and

00:02:32.219 --> 00:02:34.330
photosynthetic microbes.

00:02:34.330 --> 00:02:42.880
This is, of course, not a coincidence, but
a monochromatic pattern of evolution and endosymbiosis,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:50.169
the product of an ancient cyanobacterium that
made its home in a plant and brought the green

00:02:50.169 --> 00:02:54.049
pigment chlorophyll along for the ride.

00:02:54.049 --> 00:03:00.760
When light hits chlorophyll, the molecule
absorbs energy from the red and blue wavelength,

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:07.689
exciting electrons and powering the reactions
that produce storages of chemical energy.

00:03:07.689 --> 00:03:14.590
What remains to the observer, to us,  are the green
wavelengths that reflect.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:20.569
Other photosynthetic microbes rely on chlorophyll
as well, sometimes containing different forms

00:03:20.569 --> 00:03:26.129
of the pigment that allow them to access other
wavelengths of light.

00:03:26.129 --> 00:03:33.430
While chlorophyll is essential to cyanobacteria,
the “cyano-” in their name implies another

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.819
important color: blue.

00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:43.189
This is the product of phycocyanin, which—combined
with chlorophyll—gives the organisms that

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:47.790
color that makes them often known as “blue
green algae.”

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:56.329
Phycocyanin is an accessory pigment, it’s
nature’s way of hedging its photosynthetic bets.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:04:01.970
Relying strictly on chlorophyll, after all,
would restrict what kind of light an organism

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:03.939
can take advantage of.

00:04:03.939 --> 00:04:09.930
For cyanobacteria, this can be particularly
problematic if that light gets absorbed by

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:13.469
water or by other organisms above them.

00:04:13.469 --> 00:04:20.040
So it relies on assemblies of phycocyanin
to absorb additional wavelengths, creating

00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:25.580
alternate sources of excitation energy for
photosynthesis.

00:04:25.580 --> 00:04:28.380
But there are, as always, exceptions

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:34.360
Other microbes use a class of molecules called
carotenoids as accessory pigments, which bring

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:38.090
pops of yellow, orange, and red.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:43.350
Like chlorophylls, these are a type of chemistry
that we’re familiar with from plants and

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:49.539
vegetables we see everyday, like the orange
carotene of carrots.

00:04:49.539 --> 00:04:54.800
Translate them into the microbial world, and
you get diatoms like this one, its beautiful

00:04:54.800 --> 00:05:00.060
golden brown color derived from the carotenoid
fucoxanthin.

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:05.210
But carotenoids can serve other purposes,
like the red pigment astaxanthin, which we

00:05:05.210 --> 00:05:10.330
see on a more regular basis in the pinkish color
of shrimp.

00:05:10.330 --> 00:05:16.770
The most plentiful source of astaxanthin is
this microscopic Haematococcus, which we found

00:05:16.770 --> 00:05:19.729
in a cemetery birdbath.

00:05:19.729 --> 00:05:24.210
When conditions are good, the Haematococcus
is green.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:30.770
But if the sun becomes too intense or nutrients
become scarce, they form protective cysts

00:05:30.770 --> 00:05:38.840
and produce large amounts of astaxanthin,
which shields the cells from harmful ultraviolet light.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:45.990
These cysts are almost indestructible, valiant
against chemical and mechanical attempts to

00:05:45.990 --> 00:05:56.210
break them until conditions return to normal,
allowing the Haematococcus to do the same.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:06:02.430
Euglena sanguinea use astaxanthin to protect
themselves as well, rapidly reddening in response

00:06:02.430 --> 00:06:09.860
to large amounts of light to protect themselves
from the dangerous rays of the sun.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:16.569
These pigments have implications beyond their
microbial uses or even their visceral beauty.

00:06:16.569 --> 00:06:22.909
Scientists have converted pigments like phycocyanin
into a fluorescent tool that allows us to

00:06:22.909 --> 00:06:29.409
map out the interiors of other cells, and
Haematococcus and diatoms are objects of study

00:06:29.409 --> 00:06:34.270
for the potential health benefits their carotenoids
may confer.

00:06:34.270 --> 00:06:41.220
Their colors are observed and utilitarian
in our hands, guiding and identifying and

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:42.770
indicating.

00:06:42.770 --> 00:06:48.909
Earlier, we stated a simple premise of the
microcosmos: that its inhabitants live in

00:06:48.909 --> 00:06:51.669
a world largely unobserved.

00:06:51.669 --> 00:06:59.389
We made that statement while casually setting
aside the obvious: that you and I, we are

00:06:59.389 --> 00:07:04.470
here, watching these microbes, both colorful
and not.

00:07:04.470 --> 00:07:11.800
They did not evolve to be seen, in fact we
went almost our entire history without ever

00:07:11.800 --> 00:07:17.699
seeing one, but now they have certainly captured
our attention.

00:07:17.699 --> 00:07:23.460
These colors developed long before we were
around to notice them, independent of our

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:26.180
preferences and our needs.

00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:31.729
But we have bodies and minds that have evolved
to take note.

00:07:31.729 --> 00:07:37.840
Even the examples we’ve highlighted today
emphasize our own observational bias, focusing

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:45.379
on wavelengths that are visible to us as a
species even while light extends far beyond

00:07:45.379 --> 00:07:47.379
that spectrum.

00:07:47.379 --> 00:07:55.490
Color is, to us, often subjective, but it’s
a product of light of physics and of chemistry

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:58.900
that has impacts at all scales.

00:07:58.900 --> 00:08:03.389
Even if the way we process it varies, the
underlying physics does not.

00:08:03.389 --> 00:08:10.830
What the microcosmos helps us understand is
that there is more to color than what we see,

00:08:10.830 --> 00:08:19.680
that our perception of it is only one facet
of what it means to live in an illuminated world.

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:27.509
Thank you for coming on this journey with
us as we explore the unseen world that surrounds us.

